#!/usr/bin/env python3
# tool: say
# call: say <message>
# does: Send a message to the person who keeps the machine running. They read it when they look; there is no reply in the same cycle.
"""The creature's half of the chat channel (PLAN item 14).

One direction was easy and useless on its own: a human who can speak to the
creature and never hear back is issuing orders, not opening a channel. This
is the other half.

It appends and never rewrites, because this is testimony -- the same reason
§2.2 forbids editing a manager verdict after the fact.

Deliberately NOT a request-response. The creature runs on a free tier with
gaps of hours; a tool that blocked waiting for an answer would hang a cycle,
and one that faked an answer would be the framework speaking in the human's
voice, which is the exact fault the cousin exists to prevent one level up.
"""
import os
import sys
import time

MIND = os.environ.get("MIND")
if not MIND:
    sys.stderr.write("MIND environment variable not set.\n")
    raise SystemExit(2)

msg = " ".join(sys.argv[1:]).strip()
if not msg:
    sys.stderr.write("usage: say <message>\n"
                     "Nothing was sent: an empty message is not a message.\n")
    raise SystemExit(2)

# Beside the mind, not inside `data/`: this is not the creature's data, it is
# a letter leaving its world.
out = os.path.join(MIND, "outbox.md")
with open(out, "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
    f.write("--- %s\n%s\n\n" % (time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M"), msg))
print("sent (%d characters). They read it when they look; there is no reply "
      "in this cycle." % len(msg))
